@c ===================================================================
@node Overview
@chapter Overview

@cindex Org-Ex
@cindex widget, embedding

Org-Ex is a CMacs subsystem that extends Org mode with live, interactive
widgets.  Instead of static images or plain text, an Org buffer can
contain GTK controls (sliders, buttons, entry fields), WebKit web views,
embedded file buffers, and code blocks whose output renders inline.

The subsystem has three layers:

@table @strong
@item liborgex (C / GObject)
A shared library (@file{liborgex-1.0.so}) providing the core GObject
types: @code{OrgExWidget} (abstract base), @code{OrgExDocument}
(per-buffer manager), @code{OrgExBinding} (reactive property link),
and @code{OrgExChannel} (pub/sub messaging).  Widget subtypes include
@code{OrgExWidgetGtk}, @code{OrgExWidgetWeb}, @code{OrgExWidgetBuffer},
and @code{OrgExWidgetCode}.

@item C DEFUN bridge
@file{cmacs/org-ex/cmacs-org-ex.c} wraps every liborgex constructor and
method as an Emacs DEFUN so that all operations are callable from Elisp.
GObject return values are wrapped via @code{cmacs_gobject_wrap()}.

@item Elisp layer
Five files under @file{lisp/cmacs/} provide the minor mode, widget
type dispatch, reactive binding setup, export backends, and state
persistence.
@end table

@section Rendering Architecture

@cindex gtk-embed
@cindex rendering, GTK

Under pgtk, Org-Ex embeds native GTK3 widgets directly in the Emacs
frame using the @code{gtk-embed} xwidget type.  This is a CMacs
extension to the Emacs xwidget system (@file{src/xwidget.c}) that adds
the actual @code{GtkWidget} to the frame's @code{GtkFixed}
container---no offscreen rendering or draw callbacks needed, the widget
draws itself natively.

The rendering path for GTK widget types (@code{slider}, @code{web},
@code{elisp}):

@enumerate
@item
The widget creation function produces a @code{GtkWidget} via GObject
Introspection (e.g.@: @code{gobject-new "GtkScale"} or
@code{gobject-new "WebKitWebView"}).

@item
The widget is wrapped as an @code{OrgExWidgetGtk} via
@code{org-ex-widget-gtk-new}.

@item
A @code{gtk-embed} xwidget is created and the underlying
@code{GtkWidget} is extracted via @code{org-ex-widget-gtk-get-widget}
and set with @code{xwidget-gtk-embed-set-widget}.

@item
The xwidget is displayed via an Org buffer overlay.

@item
On redisplay, Emacs adds the widget to the frame and positions it with
@code{gtk_fixed_move}.
@end enumerate

@noindent
@strong{Note:} pgtk uses GTK3 (@code{libgtk-3}), not GTK4.  All GI
widget creation uses @code{gi-require "Gtk" "3.0"}.

@noindent
Activating Org-Ex in a buffer:

@example
;; Automatic: place this keyword in the Org file header:
#+ORGEX: t

;; Manual:
M-x cmacs-org-ex-mode
@end example

@noindent
When the mode activates it creates an @code{OrgExDocument}, scans the
buffer for @code{#+BEGIN_WIDGET} blocks, instantiates each widget, and
displays them as overlays.

@c ===================================================================
@node Widget Block Syntax
@chapter Widget Block Syntax

@cindex widget block
@cindex BEGIN_WIDGET

Widgets are declared using Org special blocks with the type
@code{WIDGET}.  The word after @code{WIDGET} on the @code{#+BEGIN_WIDGET}
line specifies the widget subtype.

@section Basic Structure

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET slider
:min 0
:max 100
:value 50
:step 1
:width 400
:height 60
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
The general form is:

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET @var{subtype}
:@var{key1} @var{value1}
:@var{key2} @var{value2}
@dots{}
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
@var{subtype} is a registered widget type name string (e.g.@:
@code{slider}, @code{web}, @code{buffer}).  Properties are lines
beginning with @code{:key} followed by whitespace and a value.

@section Common Properties

All widget types accept these properties:

@table @code
@item :width
Width in pixels (default @code{cmacs-org-ex-default-width}, 400).
May also be a percentage of the window pixel width (e.g.@: @code{50%})
or the string @code{auto} to use the widget type's default.
Percentage and auto values are resolved at display time.
@xref{Responsive Sizing}.

@item :height
Height in pixels (default @code{cmacs-org-ex-default-height}, 200).
May also be a percentage of the window pixel height (e.g.@: @code{50%})
or the string @code{auto} to use the widget type's default.
@xref{Responsive Sizing}.

@item :bind
Bind the widget to a document @code{#+PROPERTY:} line
(@pxref{Reactive Binding}).

@item :publish
Publish widget value changes to a named channel
(@pxref{Channels}).

@item :subscribe
Subscribe the widget to a named channel.

@item :direction
Binding direction: @code{both} (default), @code{to-widget}, or
@code{from-widget}.

@item :reactive
When set to @code{t}, the widget is torn down and re-created whenever
a value arrives on the channel specified by @code{:subscribe}.
Works with any widget type.  @xref{Reactive Re-evaluation}.

@item :interval
Polling interval in seconds.  The widget is periodically torn down
and re-created at this interval.  Useful for dashboard widgets that
display live data.  @xref{Timer Polling}.

@item :bridge
When set to @code{t} on a @code{web} widget, enables the JavaScript
to Elisp messaging bridge.  @xref{JS Bridge}.
@end table

@section Widget IDs

Each widget is assigned a unique ID of the form
@code{@var{subtype}-@var{position}} where @var{position} is the buffer
byte offset of the @code{#+BEGIN_WIDGET} line.  This ID is used for
document registration, state persistence, and export.

@c ===================================================================
@node Built-in Widget Types
@chapter Built-in Widget Types

@cindex widget types, built-in

Org-Ex ships with eight built-in widget types, registered at load time
by @file{cmacs-org-ex-widgets.el}.

@section slider

@cindex slider widget

A GTK3 @code{GtkScale} control created via GObject Introspection
(@code{gi-require "Gtk" "3.0"}).  The slider is embedded natively in
the Emacs frame using the @code{gtk-embed} xwidget type.

@table @code
@item :min
Minimum value (default 0).
@item :max
Maximum value (default 100).
@item :value
Initial value (default 50).
@item :step
Step increment (default 1).
@end table

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET slider
:min 0
:max 255
:value 128
:step 1
:width 400
:height 60
:bind brightness
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
The slider emits a @code{value-changed} GObject signal when the user
drags it.  When bound to a property, the corresponding @code{#+PROPERTY:}
line updates automatically.

@section web

@cindex web widget
@cindex WebKit

A WebKit2GTK web view created via GObject Introspection
(@code{gi-require "WebKit2" "4.1"}).  The @code{WebKitWebView} is a
GTK3 widget, so it embeds natively through the @code{gtk-embed} path
just like slider and elisp widgets.  Specify either a URL or inline
HTML.

@table @code
@item :url
URL to load.
@item :html
Inline HTML content (used when @code{:url} is absent).
@end table

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET web
:url https://example.com
:width 600
:height 400
#+END_WIDGET

#+BEGIN_WIDGET web
:html <h1>Hello, Org-Ex!</h1><p>Inline HTML content.</p>
:width 400
:height 200
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@section buffer

@cindex buffer widget

Embeds another file as an Emacs buffer view inside the Org document.

@table @code
@item :file
Path to the file to embed (required).
@item :editable
If @code{t}, the embedded buffer is editable.  Default is view-only.
@end table

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET buffer
:file ~/code/hello.py
:editable t
:width 500
:height 300
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@section elisp

@cindex elisp widget

Evaluates Elisp code.  The result must be a @code{GtkWidget} (wrapped
via @code{org-ex-widget-gtk-new}) or a value coerced to an HTML string
and displayed in a web view.

@table @code
@item :code
Elisp expression to evaluate (required).
@end table

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET elisp
:code (let ((btn (gi-call "Gtk" "button_new_with_label" "Click"))) btn)
:width 200
:height 50
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@section crispy

@cindex crispy widget

Evaluates a C code string via the Crispy subsystem.  The code and its
stdout output are displayed as a code widget.

@table @code
@item :code
C code to evaluate (required).
@end table

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET crispy
:code printf("result: %d\n", 6 * 7);
:width 400
:height 150
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@section bacon

@cindex bacon widget

Evaluates a shell command via the Bacon subsystem.  The command and its
output are displayed as a code widget.

@table @code
@item :code
Shell command to evaluate (required).
@end table

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET bacon
:code echo "Files:" && ls -1 ~/Documents | head -5
:width 400
:height 150
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@section wayland

@cindex wayland widget
@cindex Wayland client embedding

Embeds a live Wayland client inside the Org document.  The specified
command is spawned as a subprocess, registered for embedding via the
gowl compositor, and displayed as a gowl xwidget.

@table @code
@item :command
The Wayland client executable to spawn (required).
@item :args
Additional command-line arguments passed to the client (optional).
@end table

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET wayland
:command foot
:args --title embedded
:width 800
:height 400
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
This widget type requires the gowl Wayland compositor to be running.
The creation path is: spawn the command, register the client surface
for embedding with gowl, then create a gowl xwidget to display the
surface inline in the Org buffer.  If the gowl compositor is not
active, widget creation fails with an error.

@section image

@cindex image widget

Displays an image file inside the Org document.

@table @code
@item :file
Path to the image file (required).  Relative paths are resolved
relative to the Org file's directory.
@item :scale
Scale factor for the image (optional, default 1.0).
@end table

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET image
:file ./diagram.png
:width 400
:height 300
:scale 1.0
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
When Emacs native image support is available (built with image
libraries), the image is displayed using the native image rendering
path.  Otherwise, Org-Ex falls back to creating a @code{GtkImage}
widget via GObject Introspection and embedding it through the
@code{gtk-embed} xwidget path.

@c ===================================================================
@node Reactive Binding
@chapter Reactive Binding

@cindex binding, reactive
@cindex property binding

Org-Ex provides reactive data binding between widgets and Org document
properties.  When a widget value changes, the corresponding
@code{#+PROPERTY:} line updates; when a @code{#+PROPERTY:} line is
edited, the bound widget updates.

@section The :bind Directive

Add @code{:bind @var{property-name}} to a widget block to bind it to
a document property:

@example
#+PROPERTY: temperature 50

#+BEGIN_WIDGET slider
:min 0
:max 100
:value 50
:bind temperature
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
When the user drags the slider, the @code{#+PROPERTY: temperature}
line is updated.  When the property line is edited manually, the
slider position updates.

@section Binding Direction

The @code{:direction} property controls the binding direction:

@table @code
@item both
Bidirectional (default).  Widget and property update each other.

@item to-widget
Property changes update the widget, but widget changes do not
propagate back.

@item from-widget
Widget changes update the property, but property edits do not
propagate to the widget.
@end table

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET slider
:min 0
:max 100
:bind temperature
:direction from-widget
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@section How It Works

Under the hood, @code{cmacs-org-ex-setup-bindings} calls
@code{org-ex-binding-create} to create an @code{OrgExBinding} GObject
that connects the widget to the document.  The document monitors
@code{#+PROPERTY:} changes via @code{after-change-functions} and calls
@code{org-ex-document-notify-property-changed} to propagate.  A
re-entrancy guard (@code{cmacs-org-ex--inhibit-notify}) prevents
infinite update loops.

@anchor{Reactive Re-evaluation}
@section Reactive Re-evaluation

@cindex reactive re-evaluation
@cindex widget, reactive

When a widget specifies @code{:reactive t} together with
@code{:subscribe @var{channel}}, it subscribes to reactive
re-evaluation.  Each time a value is published on the subscribed
channel, the widget is fully torn down and re-created from its block
definition.  This is useful for widgets whose output depends on
external state communicated via channels.

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET web
:html <p>Updated at: <span id="ts"></span></p>
:width 400
:height 100
:subscribe refresh
:reactive t
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
Any widget type supports @code{:reactive t}.  The re-creation uses the
same property values from the block; to change properties dynamically,
combine reactive re-evaluation with property bindings or compute new
values in the widget's creation function.

@anchor{Timer Polling}
@section Timer Polling

@cindex timer polling
@cindex interval, widget
@cindex dashboard widget

The @code{:interval} property causes a widget to be periodically torn
down and re-created at a fixed interval (in seconds).  This is useful
for dashboard-style widgets that should refresh on a schedule without
requiring an external event source.

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET bacon
:code date +"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"
:width 300
:height 60
:interval 5
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
In this example, the bacon widget re-evaluates every 5 seconds,
displaying the current date and time.  The timer is started when the
widget is first created and stopped when the widget is torn down or the
mode is disabled.

@noindent
@code{:interval} and @code{:reactive t} can be combined: the widget
refreshes on both the timer schedule and channel messages.

@anchor{Responsive Sizing}
@section Responsive Sizing

@cindex responsive sizing
@cindex percentage width
@cindex auto sizing

In addition to absolute pixel values, the @code{:width} and
@code{:height} properties accept percentage and auto values:

@table @code
@item @var{N}%
A percentage of the Emacs window's pixel dimensions.  For example,
@code{:width 50%} makes the widget half the window width.  The value
is recalculated on window resize.

@item auto
Uses the widget type's default size or, for widgets with intrinsic
sizing (e.g.@: images), the natural content size.
@end table

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET web
:url https://example.com
:width 80%
:height auto
#+END_WIDGET

#+BEGIN_WIDGET image
:file ./photo.jpg
:width 50%
:height auto
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
Responsive values are resolved at display time and updated when the
window is resized via @code{window-size-change-functions}.

@c ===================================================================
@node Channels
@chapter Channels

@cindex channel, pub/sub
@cindex publish
@cindex subscribe

Channels provide decoupled communication between widgets that are not
bound to the same property.  A channel is a named message bus:
publishers emit values, subscribers receive them.

@section Declaring Channels

Use @code{:publish} and @code{:subscribe} in widget blocks:

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET slider
:min 0
:max 100
:value 50
:publish data-feed
#+END_WIDGET

#+BEGIN_WIDGET web
:html <div id="display">Waiting...</div>
:subscribe data-feed
:width 400
:height 100
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
When the slider value changes, it publishes the new value to the
@code{data-feed} channel.  The web widget subscribes and receives the
value via the channel's @code{::message} GObject signal.

@section Programmatic Use

@example
(let ((channel (org-ex-channel-create "my-channel")))
  ;; Subscribe:
  (gobject-connect channel "message"
    (lambda (value)
      (message "Received: %s" value)))
  ;; Publish:
  (org-ex-channel-publish channel "hello"))
@end example

@section Channel Lifecycle

Channels are created lazily on first reference (either @code{:publish}
or @code{:subscribe}).  They are stored in a buffer-local alist
(@code{cmacs-org-ex--channels}) and cleaned up when the mode is
disabled.  Channel names recorded in state files serve as markers so
that channel-based topologies are recreated on restore.

@c ===================================================================
@node Inline Widgets
@chapter Inline Widgets

@cindex inline widget
@cindex widget link

For small widgets that belong inside paragraph text, Org-Ex provides
a custom Org link type:

@example
See the current value: [[widget:slider:min=0,max=100,value=50]]
@end example

@noindent
The link format is:

@example
[[widget:@var{type}:@var{params}]]
[[widget:@var{type}:@var{params}][@var{description}]]
@end example

@noindent
@var{params} is a comma-separated list of @code{key=value} pairs.

When followed, the link creates the widget in a popup or inline
overlay.  On export, the link produces:

@table @code
@item HTML
@code{<span class="org-ex-widget" data-type="...">...</span>}
@item LaTeX
@code{\texttt@{...@}}
@item Text
The description or path string.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node State Persistence
@chapter State Persistence

@cindex state persistence
@cindex .org-ex-state

Widget state (slider positions, text entry values, toggle states) is
ephemeral---it lives only in GObject properties.  To survive buffer
kills and Emacs restarts, Org-Ex persists state to a sidecar file.

@section Sidecar File

For a file @file{notes.org}, the state file is
@file{notes.org.org-ex-state}.  The format is a Lisp alist written
with @code{prin1}:

@example
;; org-ex state file --- auto-generated
;; Do not edit manually.

(("slider-42" ("value" . "73") ("active" . "t"))
 ("web-200" ("text" . "hello"))
 (channel "data-feed"))
@end example

@section Save and Restore

State is saved automatically when:
@itemize
@item
The buffer is killed (@code{kill-buffer-hook}).
@item
@code{cmacs-org-ex-mode} is disabled.
@end itemize

@noindent
State is restored when @code{cmacs-org-ex-mode} enables: the sidecar
is read and values are applied to widgets via @code{gobject-set} after
a short timer delay (100ms) to ensure widgets have finished
initialization.

@section Configuration

@table @code
@item cmacs-org-ex-state-enabled
When non-nil (the default), enable state persistence.

@item cmacs-org-ex-state-suffix
The suffix appended to form the state file path.  Default:
@code{.org-ex-state}.
@end table

@section Cleanup

@code{M-x cmacs-org-ex-state-delete} removes the sidecar state file
for the current buffer.

@c ===================================================================
@node Export Behavior
@chapter Export Behavior

@cindex export, widgets
@cindex HTML export
@cindex text export

Org-Ex defines derived export backends so that widget blocks produce
meaningful output instead of being silently dropped.

@section HTML Export

@code{C-c C-e w h} exports the buffer using the @code{org-ex-html}
backend.  For each @code{#+BEGIN_WIDGET} block:

@enumerate
@item
If the widget is live, @code{org-ex-widget-export-html} is called,
which dispatches to the widget type's @code{OrgExExportable}
implementation.  Web widgets export their HTML directly; GTK widgets
produce a screenshot; code widgets export a @code{<pre>} block with
output.

@item
If the widget is not live (cold export without @code{cmacs-org-ex-mode}),
a placeholder @code{<div>} with the widget type and properties is
emitted.
@end enumerate

@section Text Export

@code{C-c C-e w t} exports using the @code{org-ex-text} backend.
Widgets produce a plain text description via
@code{org-ex-widget-export-text}, e.g.@: @code{[slider widget: min=0,
max=100, value=73]}.

@section PDF Export

PDF export (via LaTeX) does not have a dedicated backend.  Widgets are
exported as their text fallback descriptions.  For richer PDF output,
export to HTML first, then print to PDF.

@section Export Commands

@table @code
@item cmacs-org-ex-export-to-html
Export to an HTML file with widget support.

@item cmacs-org-ex-export-as-html
Export to an HTML buffer.

@item cmacs-org-ex-export-to-text
Export to a text file with widget fallbacks.

@item cmacs-org-ex-export-as-text
Export to a text buffer.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node Custom Widget Types
@chapter Custom Widget Types

@cindex custom widget type
@cindex widget registration

Org-Ex is extensible: you can define new widget types that integrate
with the block syntax, binding system, and export pipeline.

@section Registering a Type in Elisp

The simplest approach is to register a creation function:

@example
(cmacs-org-ex-register-widget-type "my-gauge"
  (lambda (props width height)
    ;; PROPS is an alist of ("key" . "value") strings.
    ;; Must return an OrgExWidget GObject.
    (let* ((label (or (cdr (assoc "label" props)) "Gauge"))
           (gtk-widget (gi-call "Gtk" "label_new" label))
           (widget (org-ex-widget-gtk-new gtk-widget)))
      (org-ex-widget-set-size widget width height)
      widget)))
@end example

@noindent
After registration:

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET my-gauge
:label CPU Temperature
:width 200
:height 50
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@section GObject Subclassing (C)

For full control, subclass @code{OrgExWidget} in C:

@enumerate
@item
Use @code{G_DECLARE_FINAL_TYPE} to declare your widget type.

@item
Override the virtual methods in @code{OrgExWidgetClass}:

@table @code
@item render
Render the widget to a display target.  Returns a display handle.
@item update
Update widget state from properties.
@item teardown
Release display resources.
@item save_state
Serialize ephemeral state for persistence.  Returns an
@code{OrgExWidgetState}.
@item restore_state
Restore state from a saved @code{OrgExWidgetState}.
@end table

@item
Implement @code{OrgExExportable} for export support:

@table @code
@item export_html
Return an HTML fragment string.
@item export_text
Return a plain text description.
@end table

@item
Register the type from Elisp using
@code{cmacs-org-ex-register-widget-type}.
@end enumerate

@section GObject Interfaces

Org-Ex defines two GObject interfaces that widget types may implement:

@table @code
@item OrgExRenderable
For widgets that can render to a display context.

@item OrgExExportable
For widgets that can produce HTML and text export output.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node JS Bridge
@chapter JS Bridge

@cindex JS bridge
@cindex JavaScript bridge
@cindex WebKit message handler

For @code{web} widgets created with @code{:bridge t}, Org-Ex provides a
JavaScript to Elisp messaging bridge.  This allows JavaScript code
running inside a WebKit web view to invoke Elisp handlers registered on
the Emacs side.

@section Enabling the Bridge

Add @code{:bridge t} to a web widget block:

@example
#+BEGIN_WIDGET web
:html <button onclick="sendMessage()">Click me</button><script>
function sendMessage() @{
  window.webkit.messageHandlers.cmacs.postMessage(
    JSON.stringify(@{action: "clicked", data: "hello"@}));
@}
</script>
:width 400
:height 200
:bridge t
#+END_WIDGET
@end example

@noindent
The bridge is only available for web widgets created through the GI
path (@code{WebKitWebView} via GObject Introspection), which is the
@code{gtk-embed} rendering path.

@section JavaScript Side

From JavaScript, send messages to Emacs using the WebKit message
handler API:

@example
window.webkit.messageHandlers.cmacs.postMessage(
  JSON.stringify(@{
    action: "@var{handler-name}",
    data: @var{payload}
  @}));
@end example

@noindent
The message must be a JSON string with at least an @code{action} field.
The @code{data} field is optional and can be any JSON-serializable
value.

@section Elisp Side

Register handlers using @code{cmacs-org-ex-bridge-register}:

@example
(cmacs-org-ex-bridge-register "clicked"
  (lambda (data)
    (message "Button clicked with data: %s" data)))
@end example

@noindent
When the JavaScript side posts a message with @code{action} set to
@code{"clicked"}, the registered Elisp function is called with the
@code{data} value as its argument.

@section Bridge API

@table @code
@item cmacs-org-ex-bridge-register ACTION HANDLER
Register @var{HANDLER} (a function of one argument) to be called when a
web widget sends a message with the given @var{ACTION} string.

@item cmacs-org-ex-bridge-unregister ACTION
Remove the handler for @var{ACTION}.

@item cmacs-org-ex-bridge-handlers
Return an alist of currently registered @code{(ACTION . HANDLER)}
pairs.
@end table

@noindent
The bridge file is @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-org-ex-bridge.el}.

@c ===================================================================
@node Ink Canvas
@chapter Ink Canvas

@cindex ink
@cindex Wacom
@cindex tablet
@cindex hand-drawn

The ink canvas is a tablet-driven drawing surface embedded inside Org
buffers via @code{#+BEGIN_INK} blocks.  Strokes are stored inline in
the block body as one-stroke-per-line S-expressions, so a single
@file{.org} file is self-contained, hand-editable, and produces minimal
git diffs as drawings evolve.

A modal capture window---pure GTK3 with Cairo rendering---reads pen,
eraser, and pressure straight from @code{GdkDeviceTool} and the
@code{GDK_AXIS_PRESSURE} axis.  Under standalone pgtk Emacs on Wayland,
the host compositor's @code{wp_tablet_v2} events flow through GDK
unchanged, so a Wacom tablet works without any extra configuration.

@section Requirements

@itemize @bullet
@item
SVG image support is mandatory: the rendered surface is an inline
@code{(image :type svg)} overlay.  Configure with
@code{--with-rsvg}; the build needs @code{librsvg2-devel} (Fedora /
RHEL), @code{librsvg2-dev} (Debian / Ubuntu), or the equivalent
@code{librsvg} package on Arch / macOS / FreeBSD.
@item
GTK3 (already required by pgtk Emacs).
@item
Cairo (already required by pgtk Emacs).
@end itemize

The @command{install-deps} helper at the repo root installs the right
package on every supported distribution.

@section Block Syntax

An ink block has its own header keyword vocabulary; it does @emph{not}
go through the @code{#+BEGIN_WIDGET} dispatcher.

@example
#+BEGIN_INK :id 7e3a-9b21 :w 800 :h 400 :v 1
;; cmacs-ink v1; one stroke per line for git-friendly diffs
(s :t pen :c "#222" :w 2 :p ((123 245 850) (130 250 860) (138 255 870)))
(s :t pen :c "#cc2a2a" :w 2.5 :p ((220 100 700) (228 115 750)))
#+END_INK
@end example

@table @code
@item :id
Unique block identifier.  Generated automatically on insert; used by
the write path to re-resolve the target block at commit time so a
position drift cannot cause the wrong region to be rewritten.
@item :w
Canvas width in pixels (default 800).
@item :h
Canvas height in pixels (default 400).
@item :v
Body-format version (currently @code{1}).
@end table

Each stroke line in the body has the form:

@example
(s :t TOOL :c "#hex" :w WIDTH :p ((X Y P) (X Y P) ...))
@end example

@table @code
@item :t
Tool symbol: @code{pen}, @code{eraser} (transient---never persisted),
or @code{highlighter} (reserved for v2).
@item :c
Hex colour string for the stroke.
@item :w
Base width in pixels; pressure scales it per-segment.
@item :p
List of points.  Each point is @code{(X Y P)} with @code{X} and
@code{Y} integer pixel coordinates within the canvas, and @code{P}
pressure scaled to the integer range @code{0..1000} (1000 = unknown
or no tablet---renders at full width).
@end table

Comment lines beginning with @code{;;} inside the body are ignored by
the parser and preserved on round-trip.  This is how the canonical
@samp{cmacs-ink v1} marker line lives at the top of every block body
without confusing the reader.

@section Rendering

@cindex SVG, ink rendering
@cindex variable-width strokes

When @code{cmacs-org-ex-ink-auto-render} is non-nil (default), every
block body is replaced visually with an SVG image overlay sized to
the block's @code{:w x :h}.  The renderer
(@code{org_ex_ink_render_to_svg} in
@file{cmacs/org-ex/lib/core/org-ex-ink-render.c}) emits:

@itemize @bullet
@item A white background rect so strokes stay visible against any
buffer theme.
@item A 1px @code{#cccccc} border so the canvas extent is visible.
@item One @code{<path>} fragment per stroke segment, split at
pressure-change boundaries (@code{Δwidth > 0.3px}) so single-pressure
strokes collapse to a single path.
@item Single-point strokes as @code{<circle>} so taps remain visible.
@item Eraser strokes are skipped---they are hit-test trails, never
persisted into the rendered SVG.
@end itemize

Underlying buffer text is unchanged.  @code{save-buffer} writes the
canonical S-expression form to disk; the SVG is purely a display
overlay.

@section Capture Window

@cindex capture, ink

The capture window opens as a transient GTK3 toplevel
(@file{cmacs/org-ex/cmacs-org-ex-ink-capture.c}).  It runs a nested
@code{GMainLoop} so the call returns synchronously to Elisp---the
same pattern used by the @code{xg_select} integration in cmacs-glib.

@table @kbd
@item C-RET
Commit and return strokes to the caller.
@item Esc
Cancel and discard.
@item C-z
Undo last committed stroke.
@end table

Tablet input model:

@enumerate
@item @strong{Pen tip}
@code{GDK_DEVICE_TOOL_TYPE_PEN}: appended as
@code{:t pen} in current colour and base width.
@item @strong{Eraser end}
@code{GDK_DEVICE_TOOL_TYPE_ERASER}: each stroke is a hit-test path; on
release every existing pen stroke whose points lie within
@code{max(stroke_width, 6)} pixels is removed wholesale.  Whole strokes
only---never partial pixels.
@item @strong{Side-button-as-eraser fallback}
When @code{cmacs-org-ex-ink-side-button-erases} is non-nil (default),
holding mouse button 2 mid-stroke routes through the same erase path
as @item 2.  Provides eraser support for pens that lack a dedicated
eraser end.
@end enumerate

While the modal is up, Emacs's @code{SIGALRM}-driven atimer chain is
blocked via @code{pthread_sigmask}.  Without this guard, the hourglass
timer firing during a Wacom @code{tablet-tool-added/done} dispatch can
re-enter GTK widget realisation in an inconsistent device state and
trigger a GDK assertion.

@section Marginalia

@cindex marginalia
@cindex annotation

Marginalia are per-line ink notes anchored to source code or prose
lines.  See @ref{Storage} for where annotations are persisted (org
files use an inline section by default; everything else uses a
sidecar).

@example
;; cmacs-ink/marginalia v1 - generated; safe to hand-edit.
((:format "cmacs-ink/marginalia/1"
  :file "src/foo.c"
  :anchors
  ((:id "ann-7f3a"
    :line 412
    :line-hash "9af2c0..."   ; SHA1 of line + 2 lines of context
    :width 320
    :height 120
    :created 1745600000
    :strokes ((s :t pen :c "#cc2a2a" :w 2 :p ((50 30 850) ...)))))))
@end example

@cindex line-hash
Anchors carry both a line number and a SHA1 of the line plus two
neighbours.  On load, the buffer-local list is reconciled:

@enumerate
@item Match by @code{:line}; if @code{:line-hash} matches the current
line's hash, bind to that marker.
@item Otherwise search @code{cmacs-ink-marginalia-search-radius} (10)
lines around for a hash match and relocate.
@item Otherwise mark the anchor as orphan and float it to the top of
the buffer with an @code{[orphan annotation]} badge.
@end enumerate

The sidecar is read on @code{find-file} and written on
@code{after-save} via the unified `cmacs-ink--load' /
`cmacs-ink--save' hooks installed by `cmacs-ink-mode' (storage
logic in @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-ink-storage.el}).  @code{git
status} sees source + sidecar together when annotations change.

@c ===================================================================
@node Storage
@section Storage

@cindex storage policy
@cindex inline section
@cindex sidecar

cmacs-ink persists per-line marginalia and region overlay
annotations in one of two places, chosen automatically per
buffer:

@table @strong
@item Inline section (default for org buffers)
A folded @code{* cmacs-ink :noexport:} heading is appended to
the org file with one block per anchor.  The annotation travels
with the file on copy/move; @code{git status} captures source
and annotations in a single commit; the section is excluded from
HTML/PDF exports.
@item Sidecar file (default for non-org buffers)
A `<source>.cmacs-ink' file next to the source.  Source
formats like C / Python / Markdown can't host arbitrary blocks
without breaking the parser, so the sidecar is the only option.
The sidecar is now @emph{pretty-printed} (one anchor per
indented form, one stroke per line) so it produces minimal
@code{git diff} output as strokes change.
@end table

The choice is governed by @code{cmacs-ink-storage}:

@vtable @code
@item cmacs-ink-storage
@samp{auto} (default), @samp{inline}, or @samp{sidecar}.

In @samp{auto}, org-mode buffers use the inline section and
everything else uses a sidecar.  Override per-buffer or via
@code{.dir-locals.el}.  @samp{inline} forces inline (errors when
not org-mode); @samp{sidecar} forces a sidecar regardless of
mode.
@item cmacs-ink-storage-section-name
Heading text for the inline section (default
@samp{cmacs-ink}).  Override only if it collides with an
existing heading you have.
@end vtable

@subsection Inline Section Format

@example
* cmacs-ink                                                          :noexport:
:PROPERTIES:
:VISIBILITY: folded
:END:

#+BEGIN_INK_MARGINALIA :id "ann-..." :line 412 :hash "9af2c0..." :w 320 :h 120 :created 1745600000
;; cmacs-ink marginalia v1; one stroke per line for git-friendly diffs
(s :t pen :c "#cc2a2a" :w 2 :p ((50 30 850) (60 35 870)))
#+END_INK_MARGINALIA

#+BEGIN_INK_REGION :id "rgn-..." :start-line 412 :start-col 4 :end-line 415 :end-col 12 :hash "9af2c0..." :w 480 :h 60 :created 1745600000
;; cmacs-ink region v1; one stroke per line for git-friendly diffs
(s :t pen :c "#cc2a2a" :w 2 :p ((10 5 850) (24 12 875)))
#+END_INK_REGION
@end example

Block headers use the same flat @code{:key value} parser as
@code{#+BEGIN_INK} canvas blocks; bodies use the canonical
stroke S-expr grammar produced by
@code{org_ex_ink_strokes_to_string}.  Adding a stroke is
literally one new line in the diff.

@quotation Warning
The inline section is rewritten as a unit on every save.  Any
text you put inside @code{* cmacs-ink} that is @emph{not} inside
a @code{#+BEGIN_INK_*}..@code{#+END_INK_*} block is deleted on
the next save.  Keep your own notes in a sibling heading.
@end quotation

@subsection Backward Compatibility

The loader tries the inline section first; if absent, falls
back to reading @code{<file>.cmacs-ink}.  Existing v1
single-line sidecars (one giant @code{prin1}) load cleanly --- no
flag day --- and are upgraded to the pretty-printed form (or to
inline when the buffer is org-mode) on the next save.

@subsection Migration

@cindex cmacs-ink-migrate-to-inline

@code{M-x cmacs-ink-migrate-to-inline}, run in an org buffer,
reads any existing sidecar and writes the equivalent inline
section, then prompts to delete the sidecar.  No reverse
command: to switch a buffer back to sidecar, set
@code{(setq-local cmacs-ink-storage 'sidecar)} and save --- the
inline section is removed and the sidecar regenerated.

@section Top-Level Mode

@code{cmacs-ink-mode} is a buffer-local minor mode that wires up the
hooks and the keymap.

@table @kbd
@item C-c i c
@code{cmacs-org-ex-ink-insert} - insert a new @code{#+BEGIN_INK} block
on the line below point and open the capture window.  If point is
already inside a block, escapes to the line after that block's
@code{#+END_INK} first, so the new block is never nested.
@item C-c i e
@code{cmacs-org-ex-ink-edit} - edit the block at point.
@item C-c i t
@code{cmacs-org-ex-ink-toggle-render} - flip between raw S-expr view
and the rendered SVG overlay.
@item C-c i a
@code{cmacs-ink-marginalia-add} - annotate the current line.
@item C-c i E
@code{cmacs-ink-marginalia-edit-at-point}
@item C-c i d
@code{cmacs-ink-marginalia-delete-at-point}
@item C-c i l
@code{cmacs-ink-marginalia-list} - show all annotations in the buffer.
@end table

@code{global-cmacs-ink-mode} enables the mode in any buffer that
visits a file (and any @code{prog-mode}, @code{text-mode}, or
@code{org-mode} derivative).

@section Customisation

@vtable @code
@item cmacs-org-ex-ink-default-width
@itemx cmacs-org-ex-ink-default-height
Default canvas dimensions for new blocks (800 / 400).
@item cmacs-org-ex-ink-default-colour
Default pen colour for new strokes (@code{"#222"}).
@item cmacs-org-ex-ink-default-base-width
Default pen base width in pixels (@code{2.0}).
@item cmacs-org-ex-ink-side-button-erases
Whether mouse button 2 acts as an eraser when no eraser-end tool is
detected (@code{t}).
@item cmacs-org-ex-ink-auto-render
Whether to overlay SVG rendering automatically on @code{find-file}
and after edits.  Set to @code{nil} to keep blocks visible as raw
stroke S-exprs.
@item cmacs-ink-marginalia-default-width
@itemx cmacs-ink-marginalia-default-height
Default sidecar canvas dimensions (320 / 120).
@item cmacs-ink-marginalia-default-colour
Default ink colour for marginalia (@code{"#cc2a2a"}).
@item cmacs-ink-marginalia-search-radius
Lines to scan around the recorded line for a hash match during
reconciliation (10).
@end vtable

@section Region Overlay Ink

@cindex region annotation
@cindex transparent overlay
@cindex post-glyph paint

Marginalia (@pxref{Marginalia}) attaches a single-line note as an
SVG @emph{image} overlay below the line.  Region overlay ink is a
complementary mode: select a multi-line range, draw on a screenshot
of that text, and the strokes are painted as a @strong{transparent
Cairo layer on top of the live buffer text}.  The underlying glyphs
remain editable; strokes scroll with the text and re-anchor on each
redisplay.

@subsection How It Works

This is a cmacs-only feature: it depends on a small extension to
@file{src/pgtkterm.c}.  Two paint-commit sites are intercepted; the
hook needs to fire at @emph{both} for strokes to remain stable
across cursor moves and partial redisplays:

@enumerate
@item @code{pgtk_frame_up_to_date(f)} --- catches out-of-redisplay
flushes (echo-area updates, cursor-blink via
@code{gui_update_cursor}, post-@code{force-window-update}
notifications).  The body is gated on
@code{!buffer_flipping_blocked_p()}, so it is a no-op during
@code{redisplay_internal}.
@item @code{pgtk_buffer_flipping_unblocked_hook(f)} --- the
canonical "redisplay just finished" callback during the normal
flow.  @code{redisplay_internal} wraps its work in
@code{block_buffer_flips/unblock_buffer_flips}; the
@emph{unblocked} hook is what actually performs the
@code{flip_cr_context} during a normal cursor move or scroll.
Without painting here, partial-row repaints clear stroke pixels
and the strokes never come back until something forces a
full-frame update.
@end enumerate

In both sites the hook calls @code{cmacs_ink_overlay_paint(f)},
which walks every leaf window of the frame, reads the
buffer-local @code{cmacs-ink-region--annotations} list via
@code{buffer_local_value}, and paints stored strokes on the active
Cairo context using @code{org_ex_ink_paint_strokes_cairo}.
Strokes are positioned via
@code{pos_visible_p (window, start_charpos, …)} so the overlay
tracks scroll, edits, and resize.

The capture window itself (@pxref{Capture Window}) is extended to
accept a Cairo background surface.  @code{cmacs-ink-region-annotate}
takes a screenshot of the selected region via
@code{cmacs-frame-screenshot-rect} (a new DEFUN that copies from the
frame's back-buffer with @code{cairo_set_source_surface} +
@code{cairo_paint}) and threads it into the capture as the canvas
background --- so the user is literally drawing on top of the same
pixels they will later see overlaid on the buffer.

@subsection Workflow

@enumerate
@item Activate the mode in the buffer:
@example
M-x cmacs-ink-overlay-mode
@end example
@item Select a multi-line region.
@item @kbd{C-c i r} (@code{cmacs-ink-region-annotate}).  The capture
window opens with a screenshot of the selected text as the canvas
background.
@item Draw across the text with pen / mouse.
@item @kbd{C-Return} commits.  The strokes paint on top of the
real buffer text in Emacs.
@end enumerate

@table @kbd
@item C-c i r
@code{cmacs-ink-region-annotate} - annotate the active region.
@item C-c i R
@code{cmacs-ink-region-edit-at-point} - re-edit the annotation
covering point.
@item C-c i D
@code{cmacs-ink-region-delete-at-point} - remove the annotation.
@item C-c i L
@code{cmacs-ink-region-list} - list all region annotations.
@item C-c i o
@code{cmacs-ink-overlay-mode} - toggle paint pass on/off.
@end table

@subsection Storage

Region annotations are persisted alongside per-line marginalia
through the same dispatcher --- inline @code{#+BEGIN_INK_REGION}
blocks in the @code{* cmacs-ink :noexport:} section for org
buffers, or under @code{:region-anchors} in the
pretty-printed sidecar for non-org buffers.  See @ref{Storage}
for the policy, format, and migration command.

Reconciliation on load mirrors the marginalia line-hash flow but
operates on a region: try the saved (start-line, start-col); fall
back to a hash search of @code{cmacs-ink-region-search-radius}
lines around it; orphan if no match.

@subsection Customisation

@vtable @code
@item cmacs-ink-region-default-colour
Default ink colour (@code{"#cc2a2a"}).
@item cmacs-ink-region-default-base-width
Default base pen width in pixels (@code{2.0}).
@item cmacs-ink-region-default-alpha
Stroke layer opacity in 0..1 (@code{0.85}).  The C hook reads this
on every redisplay so changes take immediate effect.
@item cmacs-ink-region-search-radius
Lines scanned for hash-based reconciliation (@code{10}).
@end vtable

@subsection Limitations

@itemize @bullet
@item pgtk only.  X11/Lucid and tty frames don't have the
post-glyph Cairo hook; the feature errors gracefully on non-pgtk
frames.
@item Heavy edits inside the annotated region don't reflow strokes;
they stay anchored to the start-marker.  Use re-edit
(@kbd{C-c i R}) to redraw on top of the new layout.
@item Pressure under @code{cmacs --gowl} is fixed at @code{1.0}
until gowl advertises @code{wp_tablet_v2} (v2 of cmacs-ink).
@end itemize

@section Limitations and Roadmap

@itemize @bullet
@item Under @code{cmacs --gowl}, pressure is fixed at @code{1.0}
(gowl does not yet advertise @code{wp_tablet_v2}; v2 lifts this).
@item @code{highlighter}, replay scrubber, gestures (scribble-out,
lasso), and OCR enrichment are reserved for v2.
@item Voice-coupled ink---time-linking strokes to libreclaw audio---is
a v3 target.
@end itemize

